bewitch (0.8.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add custom HTTP data sources -- a new Services tab that polls local services (Pi-hole, Home Assistant, Docker, etc.) and surfaces chosen numeric metrics and status badges, with history charts and Prometheus export
  * Add per-process disk I/O (read/write bytes-per-second) and per-process network I/O via eBPF (TCP rx/tx bytes-per-second, toggleable via config)
  * Add a tool-free AMD GPU backend reading amdgpu sysfs directly (no external tool required)
  * Add ECC memory alerting with ECC presence detection
  * Add a cpu.steal alert metric -- per-core steal time is now persisted and alertable
  * Add a notification send-failure count to daemon self-metrics
  * Redesign the process tab as a bounded, scrolling table over an always-visible chart, with coloured process states and pin/lightweight row markers
  * Add fired-alert multi-select clear (DELETE /api/alerts/{id}); the alerts footer now shows only the focused panel's shortcuts; clear rebound to c (x stays the screenshot key)
  * Match process pin patterns against the full cmdline, not just comm
  * Pin chart-range help to a fixed footer for CPU/Memory/Disk views
  * Harden the local API surface, packaging, and CI
  * Client config falls back to defaults silently and supports a per-user config file
  * Floor the scheduler tick to avoid runaway wakeups on coprime collector intervals
  * Cache the runtime-pins preference read off the collection path
  * Fix the Services chart blanking on metric (re)select and long Services metric names wrapping the list
  * Fix client lockout caused by the config/socket-dir tightening

 -- Ross Duggan <ross@duggan.ie>  Thu, 11 Jun 2026 10:59:17 +0100

bewitch (0.7.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add a Prometheus /metrics endpoint exposing hardware/ECC/power/SMART/GPU metrics in OpenMetrics format (honours bearer auth on TCP)
  * Add Shoutrrr notifications (Discord, Telegram, ntfy, Slack, Gotify, webhook and more) from a single shoutrrr_urls config list
  * Persist SMART disk health to a smart_metrics table and make it alertable via smart.reallocated/pending/uncorrectable/percent_used/unhealthy, with a Disk health (SMART) category in the alert form
  * Expose daemon self-metrics (write-queue depth, dropped batches, process-info cache size, heap/RSS, goroutines, per-collector backoff) on /api/stats and /metrics; bewitch stats gains a Daemon health section
  * Alerts now fire on the rising edge, suppress while breaching, and send a recovery/all-clear notification on resolve, plus a dead-man's-switch alert when metric collection stalls
  * Add a per-rule threshold aggregate (avg/max/min) so a rule can catch a transient spike or a sustained floor
  * Add system load average (1/5/15 min), per-NIC dropped packets (rx/tx), and filesystem inode usage collectors
  * Add a "daemon unreachable" indicator to the TUI status bar
  * Harden the TCP API: reject multi-statement SQL, make a tokenless plaintext (non-TLS) listener a fatal startup error, cap request bodies, set slow-loris timeouts
  * cpu.aggregate alerts now use true utilization (100 - idle, including steal) rather than just user+system of core 0
  * The alert form only offers hardware categories (SMART/GPU/temperature) the host actually reports
  * Per-view keyboard shortcuts render in a fixed footer that stays visible as content scrolls
  * A missing config file is no longer fatal -- the daemon starts with defaults
  * Fix four alert rules that advertised behaviour the engine did not implement (sustained process_down, target force-enrichment, honest threshold wording, predictive already-breached firing)
  * Fix RAPL power counters vanishing on counter wrap, and dedupe duplicate power zones discovered via flat powercap symlinks
  * Make the alert form's threshold hint metric-aware (previously showed a stale CPU label for SMART rules)
  * Guard the variance rule to memory-only metrics instead of silently running under another label
  * Make database compaction reader-safe and restart-safe
  * Stop the query API swallowing row scan/iteration errors
  * Rune-safe truncation in TUI and REPL table rendering
  * Render TUI viewport content once per message instead of twice

 -- Ross Duggan <ross@duggan.ie>  Sun, 07 Jun 2026 15:23:29 +0100

bewitch (0.6.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Active alerts are now surfaced in the TUI status bar
  * Alert rules can be edited in place (e in the alerts view, or PUT /api/alert-rules/{id}) instead of delete-and-recreate
  * Alerts view now shows a full rule detail pane, delete confirmation, and surfaced create/update errors
  * DuckDB memory is capped via a configurable [daemon] db_memory_limit (default 512MB) with spill-to-disk, instead of DuckDB's ~80%-of-RAM default that could exhaust low-memory hosts
  * Parquet archival now runs incrementally (per-day, resumable) and no longer pauses the daemon for the whole run
  * Alert rule names must now be unique, enforced by a database index; any pre-existing duplicates are auto-renamed on upgrade
  * Fix alert rules created via the API or TUI never firing (config row linked with rule_id=0 because the DuckDB driver has no LastInsertId); rules created before this fix must be recreated
  * Deleting an alert rule now also clears its fired alerts, instead of leaving orphaned undismissable alerts
  * Fix bewitchd memory leak that could OOM-kill small/low-RAM hosts -- the process-info cache is now bounded by an always-on eviction sweep regardless of retention settings
  * Fix process rss_bytes stored 1024x too large (~1 TB reported for a ~1 GB process); now correct for new samples
  * Enabling Parquet archival no longer freezes the daemon on large databases

 -- Ross Duggan <ross@duggan.ie>  Thu, 04 Jun 2026 13:23:51 +0100

bewitch (0.5.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix unbounded process_info cache growth on hosts with high process churn
  * Background goroutines (scheduled jobs, checkpoint loop) now stop cleanly on daemon shutdown instead of running against a closing DB
  * Notification sends bounded by an 8-slot semaphore
  * Intel GPU reader accumulation buffer capped at 1MB

